WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.137
Trace requests through resources and count visits.

00:00:03.137 --> 00:00:08.910
Device throughput differs from external request
throughput when a request visits repeatedly.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:14.807
Convert visits and service time into demand, then
calculate utilization and response under the

00:00:14.807 --> 00:00:15.623
chosen model.

00:00:15.623 --> 00:00:21.584
The ATM example shows why doubling arrivals near a
bottleneck can increase delay much more than

00:00:21.584 --> 00:00:22.086
twofold.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.851
Choose open, closed or mixed workload behavior
before solving the network.

00:00:26.851 --> 00:00:31.346
Fixed users with think time differ from independent
external arrivals.

00:00:31.346 --> 00:00:37.189
Map execution demands onto stations and keep per-
visit and per-request quantities distinct.

00:00:37.189 --> 00:00:43.609
Then vary uncertain parameters, identify the
limiting resource and communicate what an average-
value

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:46.948
model does not predict about bursts or tail latency.

00:00:46.948 --> 00:00:50.794
This workshop connects the two performance-modeling
units.

00:00:50.794 --> 00:00:57.426
Convert operation requests through the overhead
matrix, reduce the execution graph and obtain demand

00:00:57.426 --> 00:00:58.420
at each device.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.404
Apply arrival intensity only after that step.

00:01:01.404 --> 00:01:07.969
Check stability first, and do not hide ambiguous
branch labels: state the mapping you use or obtain

00:01:07.969 --> 00:01:09.893
it from the original diagram.
